# prod.example.invalid — THE DELTA, and deliberately nothing else.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT A COPY OF `env.staging.example` AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE.
# The environments contract says it in one line: "a divergence between the
# environments must be a visible env-var diff, never a code path" — and the
# same argument applies one level up. Two full manifests side by side would
# drift the first time somebody added a variable to one, and the drift would
# surface as a production deploy missing a setting that staging has had for
# months. So `env.staging.example` is THE manifest of names, and this file is
# the list of names whose VALUES differ, with the reason.
#
# Both are generated the same way and neither is edited by hand:
#
#     deploy/provision.sh env-file deploy/.env prod
#     deploy/provision.sh secrets-put "$DJB_DEPLOY_NAMESPACE/prod/" deploy/.env
#
# The generator takes the environment as an argument and produces every
# variable in `env.staging.example` with this file's substitutions applied.
# There is one template, so there is nothing to keep in step.
#
# LIFECYCLE. Per the environments contract, prod is EPHEMERAL in the template:
# the runbook provisions it, a backup is restored onto it, the evidence is
# taken, and it is torn down. Whether a product stands a permanent production
# environment up is the product's own ruling — the template itself never
# carries production traffic. Every value below is therefore created and
# destroyed inside one performance, and
# `deploy/teardown.sh` is a first-class part of the runbook rather than an
# appendix.

# --- The names ---------------------------------------------------------------
SITE_ADDRESS=prod.example.invalid
AUTH_ADDRESS=auth.prod.example.invalid
O11Y_ADDRESS=o11y.prod.example.invalid
ALLOWED_HOSTS=prod.example.invalid
CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://prod.example.invalid
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://prod.example.invalid
ZITADEL_EXTERNALDOMAIN=auth.prod.example.invalid
ZITADEL_ISSUER=https://auth.prod.example.invalid
ZITADEL_ADMIN_USERNAME=zitadel-admin@prod.example.invalid

# --- The database and the queue ----------------------------------------------
# A DIFFERENT TASK QUEUE IS NOT COSMETIC. Two deployments sharing a Temporal
# namespace and a queue name means either worker may accept the other's
# workflows — the exact degrade the derivation exists to delete. The two
# environments run separate Temporal servers here, and the queue is named
# differently anyway, because "they cannot reach each other" is a statement
# about today's network.
DATABASE_NAME=djb_prod
TEMPORAL_TASK_QUEUE=djb-prod
DJB_ENVIRONMENT=prod

# --- Email -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The From address is the environment's own (ADR comms-11). A production
# environment sending as `no-reply@staging.` is how a customer learns the
# difference between the two before we do.
DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL=no-reply@prod.example.invalid
SERVER_EMAIL=no-reply@prod.example.invalid

# --- The release wall's accepted fakes ---------------------------------------
# EMPTY IN PRODUCTION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DELTA. `env.staging.example` names
# the template's own two shipped comms fakes so the template's staging can
# perform its own runbook; a production environment stands on nothing it has not
# wired up, so the slot is empty and the wall is total.
#
# It is a value difference and therefore belongs in this file by its own rule
# above. If a production deployment ever needs a key here, that is a decision
# with a name and a date, and it goes in this diff where it stays visible.
#
# (MEILI_URL and MEILI_MASTER_KEY are deliberately NOT repeated below: their
# declarations are identical to staging's — one is a service name on the
# project's own network, the other is generated per environment like every other
# secret — and this file is the list of names whose VALUES differ, not a second
# manifest.)
DJB_ACCEPTED_FAKES=

# --- Secrets -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The prefix, and nothing under it is shared with staging. Not one value:
# a staging credential that also opens production makes staging — the
# environment everybody has a shell on — a production credential store.
DJB_SECRETS_SOURCE=file:/deploy/.env
# provision.sh secrets-put "$DJB_DEPLOY_NAMESPACE/prod/" deploy/.env
#
# `DJB_DEPLOY_NAMESPACE` IS NOT REPEATED HERE, AND THAT IS THE DELTA RULE
# WORKING. The namespace is the account-level slug both environments live
# under — `<ns>/staging/` and `<ns>/prod/` differ in the ENVIRONMENT segment,
# never in the namespace — so its value is identical in both and a line here
# would be a copy, not a delta. It ships EMPTY in `env.staging.example` with
# the whole argument beside it, unset is a refusal in every script that
# composes a coordinate, and this environment's value comes from its own vault
# blob like every other.

# --- Everything else ---------------------------------------------------------
# Identical to `env.staging.example`, by construction and on purpose. If a
# variable needs a different value in production, it is added ABOVE, with the
# reason, and stays visible in this diff forever.
